        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <p>OBJECTIVES:<br />
The Life Sciences, Microgravity Science and Spacelab Mission contains a number of human experiments directed toward identifying the functional, metabolic and neurological characteristics of muscle weakness and atrophy during space flight. To ensure the successful completion of the flight experiments, a ground-based pilot study, designed to mimic the flight protocols, was carried out in the head-down tilt bed rest model.</p>
<p>APPROACH:<br />
Eight healthy male volunteers the same age as the payload specialists were recruited to perform12 of the experiments planned for four individuals on the mission. Critical to the study was an integrated schedule of muscle experiments for two groups of four that allowed the experiments to be repeated at the same time of day while maintaining a normal diet schedule. Scientists with experiments accepted for the mission traveled to ARC and conducted their experiments with equipment that was similar to that to be used in space as well as before and after the 17 days of bed rest.</p>
<p>RESULTS:<br />
The intensity and frequency of exercise from the muscle testing protocols did not appear to prevent muscle atrophy and weakness. Two experiments testing calf muscle strength and fatigue characteristics with the torque velocity dynamometer showed surprisingly little effect of bed rest. At the whole muscle level, the work capacity of the calf muscle was not compromised. However, there was a decrease in the capacity of individual muscle fibers in the soleus to produce peak force, a phenomenon that could be attributed to the decrease in fiber size. The thigh muscle (quadriceps) showed an 8% decrease in the strength of a maximum voluntary contraction accompanied by a decrease in neural drive. The loss was restored by the 2nd day of recovery. Enzymes involved in glucose and glycogen metabolism as well as glycogen itself were increased, illustrating the increased dependence of type I fibers on carbohydrate metabolism. The response of a bioassayable tibial growth factor to exercise was reduced by the 2nd bed rest day and restored 3 days after the end of bed rest. Testosterone and thyroid hormones were unaffected by bed rest. Weight loss averaging 1% occurred during the first 3 days of bed rest and was promptly regained during the first two recovery days. This may have been directly related to reduced fluid intakes (250 ml/d average) and an increase in urine volume on the first bed rests day. Other elements in the diet that were spontaneously reduced by the subjects during the bed rest period were calories, protein, carbohydrate, sodium, calcium and phosphorus intakes. Total body water was reduced, but the value was not significant. The legs lost 7% lean mass with 5% regained during the first 2 days ambulation. Fluid loss during bed rest came primarily from the legs. Nitrogen balance was negative during bed rest, consistent with the decrease in lean body mass estimated by DEXA. Bone mineral density, content and body fat were unchanged. A bone marker in urine showed increased activity of bone cells during bed rest, a phenomena that continued during the recovery period. The length of the vertebral column increased by 3.5 mm at the end of 14 days bed rest, greater than the 2.7mm increase after one night and less than the 4.4 mm after long duration bed rest. Cardiopulmonary function tests revealed changes in pulmonary tissue volume (an increase) and a small but significant decrease in residual volume that appeared to be due to lung compression from the weight of the abdominal contents pressing against the diaphragm. Cardiac output, stroke volume, and heart rate were all affected by the bed test tilt and returned to standing and supine values during 1 week of recovery. Later comparison with changes in microgravity revealed that the head-down-tilt bed rest model proved to be a good model of the cardiovascular response to microgravity, but a poor model for microgravity effects on pulmonary ventilation and gas exchange. The circadian rhythm of body temperature was disrupted during head-down tilt with phase delay and a progressive flattening in a manner similar to those observed in one individual aboard the Space Station Mir. There was both subjective and objective evidence of insomnia during early bed rest and a slight increase and early appearance in REM (dreaming) sleep. The effect of bed rest on cognitive performance based on the 20 minute NASA Performance Assessment Workstation (PAWS) was minimal.</p>
